{"causes":["Peter of Toledo was educated by Islamic scholars who had recently conquered Toledo, Spain.","The Reconquista had brought Christians and Muslims into close proximity, fostering a period of cultural exchange between the two groups.","Toledo's strategic location made it a hub for trade and intellectual exchange between Europe and the Islamic world."],"effects":["Peter's translations introduced Arabic scientific and philosophical ideas to European audiences.","His work helped lay the groundwork for future thinkers like Thomas Aquinas.","The Latin translation of the Qur'an played a significant role in shaping Toledo's cultural identity during this period."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Alfonso VII","sourceName":"King Alfonso VII","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"peter-of-toledo","targetName":"Peter of Toledo","context":"During the reign of King Alfonso VII, Peter was educated by Islamic scholars who had recently conquered Toledo."},{"sourceSlug":"Toledo","sourceName":"Toledo","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"peter-of-toledo","targetName":"Peter of Toledo","context":"The city became a major center of learning and intellectual inquiry, attracting scholars from across Europe and the Islamic world."},{"sourceSlug":"Thomas Aquinas","sourceName":"Thomas Aquinas","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"peter-of-toledo","targetName":"Peter of Toledo","context":"His work helped lay the groundwork for future thinkers like Thomas Aquinas."},{"sourceSlug":"Islamic world","sourceName":"Islamic world","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"peter-of-toledo","targetName":"Peter of Toledo","context":"Toledo's strategic location made it a hub for trade and intellectual exchange between Europe and the Islamic world."}],"places":[{"name":"Toledo, Spain","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Al-Andalus","role":"Region"},{"name":"Toledo, Spain","role":"Major center of learning"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Peter of Toledo","type":"Reference article","slug":"peter-of-toledo"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3387720","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_of_Toledo"],"tags":["Physicians & Medical Pioneers","innovation-and-technology","cause-and-effect","temporal-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Peter of Toledo (died c. 1156) was a 12th-century Christian scholar and translator active in Toledo, Spain — a participant in the extraordinary translating enterprise of the Toledo School of Translators (sometimes called the Toledo Translation School), which was one of the most significant intellectual projects of the medieval world. Working in Toledo under the patronage of Archbishop Raymond I of Toledo (1124–1152), Peter of Toledo — along with colleagues including Herman the German, Dominicus Gundissalinus, and Robert of Ketton — translated works from Arabic into Latin, transmitting the accumulated knowledge of the Islamic world (including Arabic translations of Greek philosophical and scientific texts) to the Latin West.

Peter of Toledo is credited with translating Al-Kindi's 'Risāla fī ibṭāl aḥkām al-nujūm' (On the Invalidity of the Judgements of the Astrologers) and other Arabic philosophical texts into Latin — a work he undertook in collaboration with other scholars. More significantly, Peter of Toledo was involved in one of the most provocative translation projects of the 12th century: he collaborated with the English scholar Robert of Ketton on the Latin translation of the Quran — the 'Lex Mahumet pseudoprophete' (1143), the first Latin translation of the Quran, commissioned by Peter the Venerable (Abbot of Cluny) specifically to provide Christian polemicists with ammunition for arguing against Islam. This translation — although deeply polemical in intent — was nevertheless an extraordinary act of cross-cultural intellectual engagement and was the primary Latin Quran used by Christian scholars for the next four centuries.

The Toledo translators' enterprise was made possible by Toledo's unique position as a multicultural city: captured by Alfonso VI of Castile from the Moors in 1085, Toledo contained a substantial Jewish, Arab, and Mozarabic (Christian) population, and the three-way collaboration of Arabic-speaking translators, Latinate scholars, and Jewish intermediaries was the human infrastructure of the translation project. Peter of Toledo and his colleagues were at the heart of the most significant intellectual bridge between the Islamic and Christian worlds of the medieval period.","subjects":["Medieval Scholarship","Translation History","Medieval Era","Spain","Islamic Golden Age","Medieval History","12th Century","Knowledge Transfer"],"frameworks":["CULTURAL_TRANSMISSION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Peter of Toledo's work on the Latin translation of the Qur'an had a profound impact on European thought and culture, introducing Arabic scientific and philosophical ideas to a wider audience.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
